      "type": "front_running / signature replay (quote sniping & nonce-griefing)",
      "severity": "medium",
      "vulnerable_function": "beforeSwap",
      "location": "UniswapV4KEMHook.beforeSwap (also mirrors in PancakeSwapInfinityKEMHook.beforeSwap)",
      "explanation": "The signed quote digest is bound to the `sender` argument passed by the PoolManager, which in practice is the public router contract address (not the end user). As a result, anyone can copy a victim’s router calldata + signature from the mempool and submit the same swap first through the router. The hook will validate the same digest (same router `sender`, same quote terms) and then consume the nonce via `_useUnorderedNonce(nonce)`, causing the victim’s original transaction to revert when it later hits the already-used nonce. This provides no end-user front-running protection and allows cheap denial-of-service/griefing (including potentially with a minimal-sized swap, since the signature does not bind `params.amountSpecified`).",
      "attack_scenario": "1) Alice obtains an off-chain signed quote (hookData includes maxAmountIn/maxExchangeRate/nonce/expiry/signature) and broadcasts a router swap transaction.\n2) An MEV bot copies Alice’s calldata + hookData/signature from the mempool.\n3) The bot submits the same swap through the public router, optionally with a smaller `params.amountSpecified` that still satisfies `-amountSpecified <= maxAmountIn`.\n4) `beforeSwap` verifies the signature (still valid because `sender` is the router, not Alice) and consumes `nonce`.\n5) Alice’s original transaction executes later, reverts at `_useUnorderedNonce(nonce)`, and her quote is invalidated.",
      "suggested_fix": "Bind the signature to the end user, not just the router. For example, include the router’s original caller in the signed digest (e.g., `router.msgSender()` / ERC-2771-style forwarded sender) and verify it in `beforeSwap`. Additionally, consider binding `params.amountSpecified` (and any other user-controlled swap parameters like recipient) into the digest to prevent “dust swap” nonce-griefing using the same signature."
    }
  ],
  "overall_explanation": "Core logic intends to restrict swaps to signed quotes, but the signature is only tied to the router contract (PoolManager `sender`), not the end user. Because the router is public, signed quotes can be replayed by any third party, who can front-run to consume the nonce and revert the victim’s transaction. This is a concrete, exploitable mempool-sniping/DoS vector affecting all signed swaps using this hook pattern."
}
